    "desc": "Flame light sits around 1,200–1,900 K, deep amber-orange, strongest at its base and weakest at its edge, so it sculpts faces in warm pools that fall off into near-black within a meter — a physics that made firelit rooms the default theater of pre-industrial life. Painters mined it relentlessly: Georges de La Tour's candlelit saints, Rembrandt's hearth-lit interiors, and the entire Dutch genre tradition of domestic glow built their emotional temperature on it. Electric lighting replaced the hearth's practical role, and firelight became a chosen atmosphere — the 2,700 K 'warm white' standard, hygge and cottagecore fireplace imagery, and cinema's constant shorthand for intimacy and danger alike, since the same flicker reads as cozy at one scale and sinister at another.",
