    "cat": "Color & Light", "c": "Night sky illumination", "e": "historical",
    "o": "Universal; codified in Romantic painting", "g": "Universal", "p": "Antiquity–present", "era": "Romanticism to cinema noir",
    "sum": "Silver-blue night light that turns landscape and city into a mood of reverie and secret.",
    "desc": "Moonlight is physically reflected sunlight at a low intensity that human vision reads as cool silver-blue (the Purkinje shift dims reds first at night, biasing everything toward cyan-gray). That perceptual fact became an aesthetic: Whistler's Nocturnes reduced riverscapes to veils of blue-green tone; Caspar David Friedrich staged moon-gazing figures for Romantic longing; Japanese tsukimi moon-viewing built autumn rituals around it; and noir cinematography faked it with day-for-night filters that left streets indigo and glittering. In design, moonlight stands in for mystery, melancholy and calm — cool gradients, silver highlights on dark grounds, and the serene night-blue palettes of lullabies and lo-fi covers alike.",
