    "sum": "Silver airship grandeur — luxury travel at the pace of clouds, ended by one fiery evening.",
    "desc": "Count Zeppelin's rigid airships were aluminum-lattice leviathans wrapped in doped silver linen, longer than football pitches, with passenger decks inside the hull: promenades with sloping windows, lamplit dining saloons in light wood, piano music over the Atlantic. The Graf Zeppelin's 1929 round-the-world flight made them global celebrities, and their silhouette — a soft silver cigar against clouds — became the era's picture of the future: fast not by violence but by serenity. The Hindenburg disaster at Lakehurst on 6 May 1937 ended passenger airships in a single newsreel, freezing the aesthetic in amber; it has since powered every 'elegant past future' from Indiana Jones title cards to Bioshock Infinite's Columbia, the polar opposite of chrome-rocket futurism.",
