    "desc": "Hans Rudolf Giger developed 'biomechanics': beings and architecture where vertebrae become pistons, faces become engines and corridors become ribcages, executed in monochrome airbrush of oily precision. His Necronomicon portfolios (1977) led directly to Ridley Scott hiring him for Alien (1979), whose titular creature and derelict ship design won the 1980 Academy Award for visual effects and implanted the aesthetic into cinema. The style synthesizes Swiss precision, erotic-mechanical anxiety and occult mood (his spells of the Necronomicon), and it radiated outward: Beksinski's parallel visions, metal album covers, the Dark Seed games (1992), and one of tattooing's most-requested genres. Later practitioners keep the grammar — chrome skin, spinal architecture, skull-tech — in 3D renders and ZBrush sculpture.",
