    "sum": "Swords, slave arenas and twin moons — the pulp formula where chivalry survives on dying alien worlds.",
    "desc": "Planetary romance (the 'sword and planet' branch of planetary fiction) drops a capable Earthman into a decadent alien civilization where super-science is ancient, ruins are colossal, and disputes are settled with blades: Edgar Rice Burroughs' Mars books beginning with A Princess of Mars (1912) set the template — Barsoom's thin air, green Martians, airships and arena duels — and Leigh Brackett's moody Mars and Venus stories perfected its melancholic poetry for pulps like Planet Stories. Visually the formula means towering abandoned cities under unfamiliar skies, ray-guns holstered beside swords, princesses and gladiators in baroque harness, and covers (Frazetta above all) selling muscle, peril and desolation in hot oranges and purple dusk. Its DNA runs through Star Wars' desert planets, Heavy Metal comics, and every John Carter revival attempt.",
