    "cat": "Science Fiction & Fantasy", "c": "Literary fantasy subgenre", "e": "historical",
    "o": "US and UK speculative fiction", "g": "Anglophone fantastic literature and games", "p": "1930s–present", "era": "post-pulp literary fantasy",
    "sum": "Tales set at the world's exhausted end — purple skies, empty museums, magicians hoarding forgotten physics.",
    "desc": "The Dying Earth mode sets stories in a far future where the sun is guttering, science has become indistinguishable from ritual, and civilization persists as elegant decadence atop ruins: Clark Ashton Smith's Zothique tales (1950s pulps), Jack Vance's The Dying Earth (1950) with its irascible magicians Cugel and Rhialto, and Gene Wolfe's The Book of the New Sun (1980–83) that mapped the mode onto a dying Urth with baroque prose. The palette is twilight — mauve skies, jade seas, museum-cities whose artifacts outlive their meanings — and the moral weather is urbane fatalism rather than heroics. The genre gave tabletop gaming the 'Vancian' spell system (memorize, forget) and bequeathed fantasy art its wallpaper of red sunsets, tilted towers and ledger-books of dead knowledge.",
    "col": [{"h": "#8c5383", "n": "guttering sun mauve"}, {"h": "#2e3d2f", "n": "jade sea dark"}, {"h": "#c9b458", "n": "old gold leaf"}, {"h": "#4a3b52", "n": "twilight violet"}, {"h": "#d9d2c0", "n": "bone parchment"}],
