    "sum": "War-surgery splint technology bent into chairs — the sculpts of wood that taught industry comfort.",
    "desc": "Charles and Ray Eames arrived at molded plywood through failure and war: after their prize-winning but unproducible molded-plastic chairs at MoMA's 1940 Organic Design competition, they developed machine-and-vacuum plywood pressing in their Venice loft (with the Evans Products company), and when the Navy ordered leg splints with the same compound curves the technique was proven at scale. The civilian results — the LCW 'Lounge Chair Wood' (1946), which MoMA's Eliot Noyes called 'the chair of the century', and the DCW, LCM and plywood legs that later carried fiberglass shells — wrapped single sheets of walnut or ash veneer into springs of wood, their rubber-shock mounts and softly molded seats making mass production feel anatomical. The warm plywood grammar now reads as the very face of mid-century modernism and of humanized engineering.",
